= Solution
{parent=i}

The function $x^2\sin x$ is an <odd function>, since $x^2$ is even and the <sine> is odd. Its <integral> on the symmetric interval therefore cancels:
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}x^2\sin x\,dx=0.}
$$

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The double-angle identity gives $\cos^2x-\sin^2x=\cos(2x)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\pi(\cos^2x-\sin^2x)dx=\left[\tfrac12\sin(2x)\right]_0^\pi=0.}
$$

