= Solution
{parent=4}

Zero is an <eigenvalue> exactly when the <determinant> vanishes. Here $\det A=1-\alpha^2$, so
$$
\boxed{\alpha=1\quad\text{or}\quad\alpha=-1.}
$$
For either value the other <eigenvalue> is two, as follows from the <trace>.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Factorization gives $y=(x-2)^2(x+1)$, so the zeros are $x=-1$ and $x=2$, with a double root at two. The <derivatives> are $y'=3x(x-2)$ and $y''=6(x-1)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{local maximum }(0,4),\quad\text{local minimum }(2,0),\quad\text{inflection point }(1,2).}
$$
The second <derivative> changes sign at one, confirming the <inflection point>. The curve rises from $-\infty$, crosses at $-1$, reaches its local maximum, falls to touch the axis at two, then rises to $+\infty$. Neither local extremum is a global extremum on the whole real line.

\Image[paper-2-cubic-extrema.png]
{title=Zeros, two stationary points and an inflection point of the cubic}

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Expanding gives $u=2x^2+2c^2t^2$, so $u_{tt}=4c^2$ and $u_{xx}=4$. Hence $u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}$, proving that it solves the <one-dimensional wave equation>. Equivalently its two summands are oppositely travelling profiles, as in <D'Alembert's formula>.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

At $x=1$, the <natural logarithm> has value zero and <derivative> $1/x=1$. Its <tangent line> is therefore
$$
\boxed{y=x-1.}
$$

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

The logarithm must be expanded about two, not about one. Factor
$$
1+e^x=2\left(1+\frac x2+\frac{x^2}4+O(x^3)\right).
$$
Applying the supplied logarithm expansion to the bracket gives $\log2+(x/2+x^2/4)-\tfrac12(x/2)^2+O(x^3)$. Thus, through second order,
$$
\boxed{\log(1+e^x)=\log2+\frac x2+\frac{x^2}{8}+O(x^3).}
$$
In fact the cubic coefficient vanishes, since $\log(1+e^x)=x/2+\log[2\cosh(x/2)]$ and the second term is even; the <Maclaurin series> remainder can be sharpened to $O(x^4)$.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The inequality is equivalent to $|\cos\theta|>1/\sqrt2$. The <cosine> exceeds $1/\sqrt2$ close to zero and $2\pi$, and is less than $-1/\sqrt2$ close to $\pi$. Within the specified interval this gives
$$
\boxed{\theta\in[0,\pi/4)\ \cup\ (3\pi/4,5\pi/4)\ \cup\ (7\pi/4,2\pi].}
$$
The four equality angles are excluded because the inequality is strict; zero and $2\pi$ satisfy it and are included.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

For $\lambda\geq0$, the exponential <power series> gives the normalization of the <Poisson distribution>:
$$
\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{\lambda^ke^{-\lambda}}{k!}=e^{-\lambda}e^\lambda=1.
$$
For $\lambda=0$, the limiting law assigns probability one to $k=0$ and zero to every positive <integer> $k$. Negative $\lambda$ would not define the stated <probability distribution>.

