= Solution
{parent=a}

The direction <vector> is $b-a=(1,2,-2)$. The vector equation and symmetric Cartesian equation of the line are
$$
\boxed{r=(1,2,4)+t(1,2,-2),\qquad x-1=\frac{y-2}{2}=\frac{z-4}{-2}.}
$$
The $(x,y)$-plane has $z=0$, which gives $t=2$. Thus its intersection with the line has position
$$
\boxed{r=(3,6,0).}
$$

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The plane normals are $n_1=(2,-1,-1)$ and $n_2=(3,-1,-3)$. Their <cross product> is $(2,3,1)$, perpendicular to both normals and hence parallel to the intersection line. Setting $z=0$ in both equations gives the common point $(1,-1,0)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{r=(1,-1,0)+t(2,3,1),\qquad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
The normals are not parallel, so their intersection is a unique line.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

From the line point $r_0=(1,-1,0)$ to $P=(2,2,1)$, take $w=P-r_0=(1,3,1)$ and line direction $d=(2,3,1)$. <Orthogonal projection> gives the nearest parameter $t=(w\cdot d)/(d\cdot d)=12/14=6/7$. The perpendicular displacement has squared <Euclidean norm>
$$
|w|^2-\frac{(w\cdot d)^2}{|d|^2}=11-\frac{144}{14}=\frac57.
$$
Hence the <distance from a point to a line in three dimensions> is
$$
\boxed{\sqrt{5/7}.}
$$
The nearest point is $(19/7,11/7,6/7)$; subtracting it from $P$ gives a vector orthogonal to $d$, verifying the minimum.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
