Put $X=x-1$ and $H=e^{-(X^2+y^2)/2}>0$. The <gradient> components are $z_x=y(1-X^2)H$ and $z_y=X(1-y^2)H$. If either $X$ or $y$ vanishes, both must vanish. Otherwise both squared coordinates must be one. Therefore the five <stationary points> in the original $(x,y)$ coordinates are
$$
\boxed{(1,0),\quad(0,1),\quad(0,-1),\quad(2,1),\quad(2,-1).}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At $(X,y)=(0,0)$ the <Hessian matrix> is $\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$, with <eigenvalues> $1$ and $-1$, so $(x,y)=(1,0)$ is a <saddle point>. At the other four points the mixed second <derivative> vanishes and the two diagonal entries both equal $-2Xy/e$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
(x,y)&\text{type}&z\\\hline
(2,1),(0,-1)&\text{maxima}&e^{-1}\\
(0,1),(2,-1)&\text{minima}&-e^{-1}\\
(1,0)&\text{saddle}&0
\end{array}.}
$$
The four extrema are global: $|z|=(|X|e^{-X^2/2})(|y|e^{-y^2/2})\leq e^{-1}$ because each factor has maximum $e^{-1/2}$ at absolute argument one.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The zero <level set> consists of the lines $x=1$ and $y=0$. The height is positive where $(x-1)y>0$ and negative where that product is negative. In each of the four resulting regions, closed nonzero <contours> surround its extremum; levels closer to zero extend further from the extremum towards the zero-level axes and towards large distance. The height tends to zero at infinity. The figure marks the four extrema and central <saddle point>, and includes the downhill path used below.

\Image[paper-2-gaussian-contours.png]
{title=Contours, stationary points and the steepest downhill path on the Gaussian-weighted surface}

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the graph surface $Z=z(x,y)$, an upward normal is $(-z_x,-z_y,1)$. At $x=y=1$, let $q=e^{-1/2}$; then $z_x=q$ and $z_y=0$. Normalizing gives the <unit normal vector>
$$
\boxed{n=\frac{(-q,0,1)}{\sqrt{1+q^2}}=\frac{(-e^{-1/2},0,1)}{\sqrt{1+e^{-1}}}.}
$$
Its negative is the equally valid downward normal.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A tangent displacement has the form $(dx,dy,z_xdx+z_ydy)$. At the specified point, $z_y=0$ and $z_x=q>0$, so the fastest decrease of height per unit surface distance has $dy=0$ and $dx<0$. The unit downhill tangent is $(-1,0,-q)/\sqrt{1+q^2}$. Therefore, for speed $u$ measured along the surface,
$$
\boxed{v=-\frac{u}{\sqrt{1+e^{-1}}}(1,0,e^{-1/2}).}
$$
Its <Euclidean norm> is $u$, and it is orthogonal to the surface normal. The general construction is <steepest descent on a graph surface>; using a horizontal speed equal to $u$ would overestimate the full three-dimensional speed.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The horizontal direction of <steepest descent on a graph surface> is parallel to $-\nabla z$. On the line $y=1$, the component $z_y=X(1-y^2)H$ vanishes identically, so that line is invariant under the downhill path. Starting at $X=0$, $z_x=(1-X^2)e^{-(X^2+1)/2}>0$ for $-1<X<0$, so $x$ decreases towards zero. The path reaches the minimum
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(0,1,-e^{-1}).}
$$
At the minimum no strictly downhill direction remains. A constant-speed parametrization reaches it after the finite arc length divided by $u$; the unnormalized <gradient flow> approaches the same endpoint only asymptotically in its own time parameter.

