Expanding the <determinant> along the first row gives
$$
\boxed{\det A=1(-2-1)-2(0+1)+(-1)(0-1)=-4.}
$$
In particular the matrix is invertible.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <cofactor matrix> is $\begin{pmatrix}-3&-1&-1\\5&-1&3\\-1&1&1\end{pmatrix}$. Transpose it to form the <adjugate matrix>, and divide by the determinant $-4$:
$$
\boxed{A^{-1}=\frac14\begin{pmatrix}3&-5&1\\1&1&-1\\1&-3&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Direct matrix multiplication gives $AA^{-1}=I$, checking all entries.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A homogeneous square <linear system> has a nonzero solution exactly when its coefficient matrix is singular. Here
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\1&2&0\\1&-3&\lambda\end{pmatrix}=\lambda-5.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\lambda=5.}
$$
For this value, the second equation gives $x=-2y$ and the first gives $z=y$; the third then holds automatically. All solutions are $t(-2,1,1)$, with $t\ne0$ for the nonzero ones.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The coefficient matrix is a <Vandermonde matrix>, with determinant
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\a&b&c\\a^2&b^2&c^2\end{pmatrix}=(b-a)(c-a)(c-b).
$$
Therefore a nonzero solution exists exactly when
$$
\boxed{a=b\quad\text{or}\quad a=c\quad\text{or}\quad b=c.}
$$
For example if $a=b$, the first two columns are equal and $(1,-1,0)$ is a solution. If exactly two parameters agree, the distinct remaining column is independent of the repeated one, so the <kernel> has dimension one. If all three agree, only $x+y+z=0$ remains, giving a two-dimensional <kernel>.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the real <Fourier series> convention $g=a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}[a_n\cos(n\pi x)+b_n\sin(n\pi x)]$ for period two. Since $g$ is an <even function>, $b_n=0$, and $a_0=\int_{-1}^1x^2dx=2/3$. Twice applying <integration by parts>, with $k=n\pi$, gives
$$
a_n=2\int_0^1x^2\cos(kx)dx
=2\left[\frac{x^2\sin(kx)}k+\frac{2x\cos(kx)}{k^2}-\frac{2\sin(kx)}{k^3}\right]_0^1
=\frac{4(-1)^n}{\pi^2n^2}.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{g(x)=\frac13+\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2}\cos(n\pi x).}
$$
The periodic extension is continuous at its endpoints, so the series takes the specified endpoint values as well as the interior values.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Apply <Parseval's identity> in the same coefficient convention:
$$
\int_{-1}^1g(x)^2dx=\frac{a_0^2}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^\infty(a_n^2+b_n^2).
$$
The left-hand side is $\int_{-1}^1x^4dx=2/5$. Thus $2/5=2/9+(16/\pi^4)\sum_{n\geq1}n^{-4}$, giving
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac1{n^4}=\frac{\pi^4}{90}.}
$$

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Again the function is even, so all sine coefficients vanish. Its constant term is $a_0/2=\sinh1$. With $k=n\pi$, an <antiderivative> is
$$
\int\cosh x\cos(kx)dx=\frac{\sinh x\cos(kx)+k\cosh x\sin(kx)}{1+k^2}.
$$
Thus $a_n=2(-1)^n\sinh1/(1+n^2\pi^2)$. In the requested factored notation,
$$
\boxed{A_0=1,\qquad A_n(x)=\frac{2(-1)^n\cos(n\pi x)}{1+n^2\pi^2}.}
$$
This is the period-two specialization of the <Fourier series of periodic hyperbolic sine and cosine>. The function values match at the endpoints, although its derivative has a jump in the periodic extension.

