= Solution
{parent=i}

In <Cartesian coordinates>, the <divergence> is the sum of the three component derivatives. Therefore
$$
\nabla\cdot r=\frac{\partial x}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial y}{\partial y}+\frac{\partial z}{\partial z}=3.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}>0$, the <chain rule> gives $\partial_x(r^{-1})=-x/r^3$, with analogous formulas for the other components. Hence
$$
\boxed{\nabla(1/r)=-\frac{r}{r^3}.}
$$
On the right the numerator denotes the position vector, while its denominator is the cube of its magnitude.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The vector $m$ is constant, so $\nabla(m\cdot r)=m$. Also $\nabla(r^{-3})=-3r/r^5$, by the same <chain rule>. The <product rule> therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\nabla\left(\frac{m\cdot r}{r^3}\right)=\frac m{r^3}-\frac{3(m\cdot r)r}{r^5},\qquad r>0.}
$$
The three components follow by differentiating $(\alpha x+\beta y+\gamma z)(x^2+y^2+z^2)^{-3/2}$.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For constant $m$, the <curl of a cross product> identity reduces to
$$
\nabla\times(m\times\nabla\phi)=m\Delta\phi-(m\cdot\nabla)\nabla\phi.
$$
Commuting mixed second <partial derivatives> gives $(m\cdot\nabla)\nabla\phi=\nabla(m\cdot\nabla\phi)$. Hence the requested sum equals $m\Delta\phi$, which is zero because $\phi$ is <harmonic>. The argument assumes the indicated second derivatives are continuous on the domain.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

From the preceding gradient, $\partial_x^2(r^{-1})=-r^{-3}+3x^2r^{-5}$. Summing the three Cartesian second <partial derivatives> gives
$$
\Delta(1/r)=-3r^{-3}+3(x^2+y^2+z^2)r^{-5}=0,\qquad r>0.
$$
Thus the reciprocal radius is <harmonic> away from the origin. Its negative gradient is
$$
\boxed{E=\frac{r}{r^3},\qquad\nabla\times E=0\quad(r>0).}
$$
The <curl> vanishes because $E$ is a gradient field and mixed second derivatives commute. The exclusion of the origin is essential: the potential and field are singular there.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
