= Solution
{parent=a}

For a continuous <random variable> with <probability density function> $p$, its <expected value> and <variance> are
$$
\boxed{\mu_X=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}xp(x)dx,\qquad\operatorname{Var}X=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}(x-\mu_X)^2p(x)dx.}
$$
The mean requires an integrable first absolute moment, and a finite variance requires a finite second moment. When these hold, $\operatorname{Var}X=\mathbb E[X^2]-(\mathbb EX)^2$.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take $\sigma>0$ and substitute $u=(x-\mu)/\sigma$. The density becomes the standard <Gaussian distribution> density $\varphi(u)=e^{-u^2/2}/\sqrt{2\pi}$. Its normalization follows from the supplied <Gaussian integral> after scaling by $\sqrt2$. Since $u\varphi(u)$ is an integrable <odd function>, its integral is zero, and therefore $\mathbb EX=\mu$.

For the second moment, $\varphi'=-u\varphi$. <Integration by parts> gives
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}u^2\varphi(u)du=-[u\varphi(u)]_{-\infty}^{\infty}+\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\varphi(u)du=1.
$$
The endpoint term vanishes by Gaussian decay. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EX=\mu,\qquad\operatorname{Var}X=\sigma^2.}
$$

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <conditional probability> product rule gives $P(A\mid B)P(B)=P(A\cap B)$. Applying it once more gives
$$
\boxed{P(A\mid B)P(B)P(C\mid A\cap B)=P(A\cap B\cap C).}
$$
The conditionals in the original expression require their conditioning events to have positive probability.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The events $B$ and $\overline B$ form a partition. The <law of total probability> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{P(A\mid\overline B)P(\overline B)+P(B)P(A\mid B)=P(A).}
$$
As written, both conditionals require nonzero conditioning probabilities.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The numerator $P(B\mid A)P(A)$ equals $P(A\cap B)$, while $P(A\mid B)=P(A\cap B)/P(B)$. Thus, wherever the original quotient is defined,
$$
\boxed{\frac{P(B\mid A)P(A)}{P(A\mid B)}=P(B).}
$$
In particular a zero numerator and denominator cannot be cancelled; the original expression requires $P(A\mid B)>0$. This is the rearrangement of <Bayes' theorem>.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The two-event <inclusion-exclusion principle> subtracts the overlap counted twice:
$$
\boxed{P(A)+P(B)-P(A\cap B)=P(A\cup B).}
$$

= v
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

This is exactly the definition of <conditional probability>, provided $P(A)>0$:
$$
\boxed{\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(A)}=P(B\mid A).}
$$

= d
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Under a uniform shuffle, every unordered five-card hand is equally likely, giving $\binom{52}{5}$ possible hands. Choose the triple's rank in 13 ways and its suits in $\binom43$ ways; then choose a different pair rank in 12 ways and its suits in $\binom42$ ways. Each full house determines these choices uniquely, so there is no further ordering factor. Therefore
$$
\boxed{N=13\binom43\,12\binom42=3744,\qquad P=\frac{3744}{\binom{52}{5}}.}
$$
The count uses <binomial coefficients> because the order of cards within a hand is irrelevant.

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The integrand is continuous on the compact rectangle, so <Fubini's theorem> allows integrating in $y$ first. For $x\ne0$, $\int_0^1xe^{xy}dy=e^x-1$; the same formula holds by continuity at $x=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1\int_0^2xe^{xy}dx\,dy=\int_0^2(e^x-1)dx=e^2-3.}
$$

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <domain of integration> is the upper half of the <ellipse> $x^2+4y^2\leq1$. Reversing the order makes $-1\leq x\leq1$ and $0\leq y\leq\tfrac12\sqrt{1-x^2}$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\int_{-1}^1\int_0^{\sqrt{1-x^2}/2}y\,dy\,dx=\frac18\int_{-1}^1(1-x^2)dx=\frac16.}
$$
The inner antiderivative is $y^2/2$, so the square of the upper limit removes the square root.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use <cylindrical coordinates> $x=r\cos\theta$, $y=r\sin\theta$. The upper plane gives $z=4-r(\cos\theta+\sin\theta)>0$ throughout the quarter disk, and the <Jacobian determinant> contributes $r$. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
V&=\int_0^{\pi/2}\int_0^1[4-r(\cos\theta+\sin\theta)]r\,dr\,d\theta\\
&=\int_0^{\pi/2}\left[2-\frac{\cos\theta+\sin\theta}{3}\right]d\theta,
\end{aligned}
$$
so
$$
\boxed{V=\pi-\frac23.}
$$

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
